You asked for a small internal system, and the quote came back at RM 30,000.
Your first thought was probably that a developer's salary is nowhere near
that, so somebody is padding the bill.

Sometimes somebody is. More often the number is just arithmetic nobody showed
you. This post does that arithmetic in the open, using published prices and
public salary data, so you can run the same check on any quote you're holding,
including ours.

## How much does custom software cost in Malaysia?

Most Malaysian software houses won't put a number on their website. A few do,
and those are the only figures worth comparing:

| Vendor | Published entry price | Published timeline |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Nexawork AI Starter Pack | From RM 19,900 | Delivery date written into the contract |
| Nexvance Starter | RM 15,000–30,000 | 4–6 weeks |
| Corpcode Lite | From RM 30,000 | 6–10 weeks |

Nexawork's figure comes from its
[launch announcement of 9 November 2026](https://www.malaymail.com/news/money/mediaoutreach/2026/11/09/nexawork-ai-launches-custom-software-for-smes-from-rm1990000/499935),
which describes a first system built around one core workflow, with a full
refund of the development fee if the contracted delivery date is missed.
Nexvance's range is on its
[pricing page](https://nexvancetech.pages.dev/pricing) and includes six months
of post-launch support.

Ours is on the
[Corpcode page](/corpcode?utm_source=blog&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=corpcode-blog-custom-software-development-cost-malaysia&utm_content=cta-inline-en-a):
Lite builds from RM 30,000, Standard from RM 60,000, Advanced from RM 150,000
and Enterprise from RM 300,000. Those figures exclude 8% SST and pass-through
costs such as hosting and third-party APIs, so a RM 30,000 build is RM 32,400
on the invoice. Paid discovery is listed as its own step, from RM 5,000 over
one to four weeks.

So the honest answer to "how much" is that a single-purpose system for one
team starts somewhere between RM 15,000 and RM 30,000, depending on who
builds it and how many weeks they spend. The more useful question is why.

## Why is custom software so expensive?

Because you're renting people, and people in this trade are not cheap. Here
is the sum, line by line.

Second Talent's
[Malaysia developer rate card](https://www.secondtalent.com/developer-rate-card/malaysia/),
drawn from job ads and current to August 2026, puts a mid-level developer at
RM 5,500–9,000 a month. On wages above RM 5,000 the employer adds 12% EPF
([malaysia.gov.my](https://www.malaysia.gov.my/my/personas/tenaga-kerja-muda/hak-dan-manfaat-pekerja/caruman-kwsp-sebagai-hak-pekerja)).

| Line | Sum | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mid-level developer, salary | Second Talent rate card | RM 5,500–9,000 a month |
| Plus employer EPF | Salary × 1.12 | RM 6,160–10,080 a month |
| Same developer, per week | Monthly × 12 ÷ 52 | RM 1,420–2,330 a week |
| That developer for a 6–10 week build | Weekly × 6, weekly × 10 | RM 8,500–23,300 |
| What's left of RM 30,000 | RM 30,000 minus the line above | RM 6,700–21,500 |

That last line has to pay for everything that isn't one developer typing:
scoping, interface design, testing, project management, deployment, the
warranty period, SOCSO and EIS, laptops and software, the weeks the vendor
spent on proposals that didn't close, and a margin.

This is not Corplabs' internal cost sheet. It's a floor you can rebuild from
public numbers, and it explains why published prices cluster where they do.
When a quote lands well under those entry prices, the vendor is either
assigning less than one experienced person, reusing something already built,
or losing money. The first two can be perfectly fine. You just want to know
which one you're buying.

One more comparison. Hiring that mid-level developer yourself costs RM 73,920
to RM 120,960 a year in salary and EPF alone, before you've worked out who
will manage them. A RM 30,000 build is three to five months of that hire.

## Can I get a cheaper rate?

You can get a cheaper total. You'll rarely get a cheaper rate, and here's the
check that shows it: divide any quote by its number of weeks.

- Nexvance Starter, RM 15,000–30,000 over 4–6 weeks: RM 2,500–7,500 a week.
- Corpcode Lite at its floor, RM 30,000 over 6–10 weeks: RM 3,000–5,000 a
  week.

The weekly figures overlap. The entry prices differ mostly because the
builds are different lengths. Comparing vendors by their "from" price hides
this. A lower quote usually means fewer weeks, and fewer weeks means less
software.

That tells you where your negotiating power is. Asking for 20% off the same
scope gets you either a no or a quiet cut in testing. Cutting scope gets you
a lower number that holds. Three cuts that shorten a build:

- **Drop the roles you don't need on day one.** Every extra permission level
  is screens, rules and test cases.
- **Keep one integration, not three.** Export a CSV to your accounting
  software until the volume justifies a live connection.
- **Skip the mobile app.** A web app that works on a phone browser covers
  most internal use.

This is also what
[paid discovery](/corpcode?utm_source=blog&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=corpcode-blog-custom-software-development-cost-malaysia&utm_content=cta-inline-en-b)
is for. From RM 5,000 you get a written scope of work, an architecture
diagram and a fixed quote, and they're yours to keep whether or not we build
it. You can take that scope to two other vendors and compare like with like,
which is hard to do with three proposals written from three different
conversations.

## How long does custom software take?

For the smallest tier, the published figures say four to ten weeks of build:
4–6 weeks at Nexvance, 6–10 at Corpcode Lite. Add one to four weeks if the
vendor runs a separate scoping phase, as we do. Larger systems are a
different order: Corpcode's Standard tier is listed at three to five months.

Whichever vendor you pick, ask how often you'll see working software.
Corpcode's published process is a demo every two weeks, with your sign-off
before each payment milestone.

## Do I really need the maintenance?

For the first weeks, no. Corpcode includes a 60-day warranty after launch,
and Nexvance publishes six months of support. Ask every vendor for that
number.

After that, you need someone, though not necessarily the vendor who built it.
Software that nobody touches still ages. Node.js, a common runtime for
business web apps, gives each long-term-support version about
[30 months of critical fixes](https://nodejs.org/en/about/previous-releases).
Version 20 came out in April 2023 and is already end-of-life. A system
launched today on a current version will need a runtime upgrade within three
years, and so will the libraries, the hosting and any API it talks to.

The rule of thumb is that maintenance runs 15–20% of the build cost a year
([CodeStringers](https://www.codestringers.com/articles/software-maintenance-costs-explained),
[Space-O](https://www.spaceo.ca/blog/software-maintenance-cost/)). On a
RM 30,000 build that's RM 4,500–6,000 a year, or RM 375–500 a month. It's a
rule of thumb and not a law. One benchmark of 24 software products puts a
quiet, low-risk internal app nearer 10–16%
([Wavect](https://wavect.io/blog/software-maintenance-cost-benchmark-dach-saas/)).

Two things to settle before you sign the build, not after:

1. **The retainer price, in writing.** Corpcode's retainer isn't on the
   website because it depends on what gets built. It should be in your
   discovery documents.
2. **Who holds the source code.** By default Corplabs keeps ownership and you
   get a perpetual licence to use the system. Full source transfer is a paid
   add-on. If you want the freedom to hand maintenance to a freelancer or
   your own hire later, buy the transfer and budget for it up front. Ask
   every other vendor the same question before you compare prices.

## When is custom software not for you?

Quite often. Don't commission a build if:

- **An existing product already does it.** If what you need is invoicing,
  inventory or accounting, that's an ERP. A RM 30,000 build costs the same as
  30 months of
  [Corprise](/corprise?utm_source=blog&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=corprise-blog-custom-software-development-cost-malaysia&utm_content=cta-inline-en-a)
  at its RM 1,000 a month entry tier, and
  the ERP arrives with those modules already written.
- **The process is still changing.** If the workflow lives in one person's
  head and shifts every month, stay on a spreadsheet for another quarter.
  Software fixes a process in place, including the bad parts.
- **The job is small and you can test it yourself.** One form, one report,
  one script: a good freelancer will cost less than any software house,
  because you're paying for one person without the project manager, the
  tester or the warranty. That's the right call when the tool isn't
  business-critical and you could live with that person being unreachable
  for a month.
- **You need it in two weeks.** No published timeline above starts that low.

Custom software earns its price when the process is stable, specific to how
you operate, used every day, and badly served by the products you've tried.

## What should you do next?

Take the quote you already have and do two sums: divide it by its weeks, and
take 15–20% of it for each year after launch. If both numbers make sense for
what the system will save you, you have a real project.

If you'd like a second opinion on that scope, use the
[Corplabs contact form](/contact?utm_source=blog&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=corpcode-blog-custom-software-development-cost-malaysia&utm_content=cta-banner-en-a).
It asks for your name, your email and a few lines on what you're building. We
usually reply within a business day, and the first discovery call is free.
Paid discovery only starts if you decide you want the written scope and fixed
quote. If a freelancer or an off-the-shelf tool is the better answer, that's
what you'll hear on the call.
